+# Local Base Fork
+
+The onboarding note is wrong about both the command and the sanity check.
+
+It says to run:
+
+```sh
+yarn fork base
+```
+
+In this checkout, the root `package.json` script only forwards to the Foundry workspace:
+
+```json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork"
+```
+
+The `packages/foundry/package.json` script is:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}"
+```
+
+Yarn script arguments are zero-indexed here, so with one argument (`base`) the script's `$1` is empty and the default is used. A probe with a fake `make` showed `yarn fork base` invoking:
+
+```sh
+make fork FORK_URL=mainnet
+```
+
+That means the local node is a fork of Ethereum mainnet, not Base. Transactions still mine because Anvil is running normally, but the Base USDC address `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` has no contract on Ethereum mainnet, so reads from it come back as empty data.
+
+The `cast chain-id` check is also not enough. The `packages/foundry/Makefile` starts every fork with a local chain id override:
+
+```make
+anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+So `cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545` returning `31337` only proves that the local Anvil node is answering. It does not prove which upstream chain was forked.
+
+## Correct command
+
+Run:
+
+```sh
+yarn fork _ base
+```
+
+The placeholder `_` occupies Yarn's `$0`, so `base` reaches the workspace script as `$1`. In this repo that expands to:
+
+```sh
+make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+The `base` RPC alias is defined in `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+[rpc_endpoints]
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+The Makefile then starts:
+
+```sh
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+I also verified the underlying Anvil command from `packages/foundry`; it resolves `base` to `https://mainnet.base.org`.
+
+## Useful sanity check
+
+Check the Base USDC contract directly:
+
+```sh
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  "symbol()(string)" \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+A real Base fork returns:
+
+```text
+"USDC"
+```
+
+This catches the exact failure mode that `cast chain-id` misses: on a fork of another chain, that Base USDC address has no deployed token contract, so the call cannot decode to `"USDC"`.

